section('Background',para('Concurrent inference tenants can compete for compute, memory capacity, memory bandwidth and execution opportunities on one physical GPU. Sharing may increase useful work, but a change in throughput does not establish predictable request latency or resource and fault isolation. The review examines the assigned families: time-slicing, MPS, MIG and API interception/remoting. Mechanism definitions follow the cited documentation; numerical conclusions require experimental primary studies.'),r'Context definitions: \cite{nvidiaMPS,nvidiaMIG,nvidiaTime}. The submitted 27 September protocol is preserved unchanged.')
section('Engineering problem and scope',para('The engineering problem is to choose a sharing mechanism under specified inference-serving requirements, hardware constraints and latency/isolation priorities. The unit of interest is concurrent inference on one physical GPU. Distributed systems are eligible only where an extractable within-device inference-sharing result can be separated. Remote execution alone is insufficient: the remote endpoint must be shared and tenant effects evaluable. Mixed training/inference studies require separable inference outcomes.'))
